to form two Chows, which would give me two more melds, plus the existing one. Then, with the remaining tiles, I need a pair. \n\nAlternatively, if I play T7, maybe I can also get B6 to form another meld. But B6 is a single. \n\nSo the best action seems to be playing T7 to keep the T4-5-6 Chow possibility. Therefore, the answer is Play T7.\n", "output": "Evaluation: Playing T7 allows forming a Chow with T4, T5, T6, and preserves potential pairs in F2.  \nReason: By discarding T7, the remaining T4, T5, T6 can form a Chow, and F2 remains for a possible pair. This maintains flexibility for completing the hand with additional melds and a pair.  \nAnswer: Play T7"}
